// Practice quiz: if/else and switch
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, so comparisons and switch arrows are written in words.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/java-basics/if-else-and-switch

::o1 first true branch::A chain tests if (isAdmin), then else if (isMember), then else. Which branch runs for a user who is both an admin and a member? {
=Only the isAdmin branch#Correct. Java runs the first branch whose condition is true and skips the rest of the chain.
~Both the isAdmin and the isMember branches#Not quite. An if / else if chain runs at most one branch.
~Only the isMember branch, because it is more specific#Not quite. Java does not judge specificity; it takes the first true condition from the top.
~The else branch#Not quite. else runs only when every condition above it is false.
}

::o1 order the thresholds::A grade chain has two conditions, score at least 60 and score at least 90. Which order gives a score of 95 the grade for 90 or more? {
=Test score at least 90 first, then score at least 60#Correct. Put the narrowest condition first; 95 then stops at the 90 branch.
~Test score at least 60 first, then score at least 90#Not quite. 95 is at least 60, so it would stop at the first branch.
~The order does not matter#Not quite. Only the first true branch runs, so the order decides the result.
~Use two separate if statements instead of else if#Not quite. Both would run for 95, and the later one would overwrite the grade.
}

::o2 switch expression coverage::A switch expression over an int lists case 1 and case 2 but has no default. What happens? {
=It does not compile, because not every int value is covered#Correct. A switch expression must yield a value for every input, so it needs default here.
~It yields null for other values#Not quite. The compiler rejects it before it can run.
~It falls through to the next statement#Not quite. Switch expressions never fall through.
~It yields the value of the last case#Not quite. Without default, other inputs have no value at all, so it is an error.
}

::o2 several values::In an arrow-label switch, how do you let one branch handle both 6 and 7? {
=Write case 6, 7 followed by the arrow#Correct. Arrow labels can list several values separated by commas.
~Write two labels, case 6 and case 7, and rely on fall-through#Not quite. Arrow labels never fall through.
~Write case 6 or 7#Not quite. Java uses a comma, not the word or.
~Write case 6..7#Not quite. Java case labels have no range syntax.
}

::o3 fall-through::A colon-label switch has case 1, case 2 and case 3, each printing one word and none with break. What prints when the value is 1? {
=All three words, in order#Correct. Java jumps to case 1 and falls through every later case because nothing stops it.
~Only the case 1 word#Not quite. That needs a break at the end of case 1.
~Nothing, because break is missing#Not quite. A missing break does not stop the match; it lets execution continue.
~Only the case 3 word#Not quite. Execution starts at the matching label, case 1.
}

::o3 placing break::Where does break go to stop an old-style case from falling through? {
=After the last statement of that case, before the next case label#Correct. break leaves the switch, so the following cases do not run.
~Before the case label#Not quite. Statements before a label belong to the previous case.
~Only after default#Not quite. That would leave every other case falling through.
~Nowhere: colon labels never fall through#Not quite. Colon labels do fall through; arrow labels do not.
}
